## Broken

| Status | Defect | Symptom | References |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| todo | **An embedded card's Markdown links resolve from the host page.** Expansion is textual and carries a wikilink back to the card it was written in, but an ordinary relative link is plain text to it and is resolved from the page that embeds it | A link, an image, or a path to a card written in an embedded card is wrong on every host in another directory, on the site and in `hmd render`. MkDocs reports it at `info` only, so a strict build stays green | [the issue card](../issues/0012-embedded-markdown-links-resolve-from-the-host-page.hmd), [[hmd-0001#6-embed-expansion]] |
| blocked | **The retired domain answers 404 instead of redirecting.** Its records point at a forwarding service, so the registrar side is half-configured, but no rule takes effect on either scheme | Every link published under the old host is dead. The fix is a web form, not a commit | [[hmd-0005#the-canonical-host-and-the-state-dns-has-to-reach]] |
| todo | **A heading that ends in a code span loses that span.** The scanner measures where a heading ends on the masked text, in which inline code is blank, so code at the end of a heading is cut off before the heading's text is read. Code in the middle of a heading is kept | A reference to the proposal heading *4. Red links and .md*, whose last word is a code span, is reported as a missing heading (HMD004) unless the fragment leaves that word out. A tracker titled *STATUS* followed by a code span is reported as a duplicate of its own *Status* section (HMD011). Five tracker references are written without their last word so that the linter passes: `#4-red-links-and` in four places and `#the-divergence-with` in one. They must gain the word back when this is fixed, and the linter will report them then | [[hmd-lang-spec#heading-anchors]] |
| todo | **A code span that contains three backticks opens a fenced region.** A span written with single backticks around three backticks is read as the start of a fence, which runs to the next such span, across paragraphs | Between two such spans, a wikilink shown inside a code span is read as a real wikilink and reported as unresolved (HMD001). Found in the requirements sketch under `doc/models/`, which now writes those two spans with character references | [[hmd-lang-spec#lexical-model]] |

## Limitations (known gaps)

| Limitation | Why it stands | References |
| --- | --- | --- |
| The workspace index reads every card from disk and cannot be handed an editor's unsaved buffer | Being fixed — it is the first open item above. Worth knowing that it is one layer, not a rewrite: the functions beneath it already take text | [[hmd-0024#text-not-paths]] |
| The TypeScript implementation solved that years earlier in project time, and this one did not | Not a defect, an asymmetry: that one reads through an injectable port because it had to. This one never had a caller that needed it | [[hmd-0020#6-resolver]] |
| The source archive does not ship the example vaults, so linting them does not work from an unpacked archive | The examples are repository fixtures that both implementations lint, with exactly one copy at the root. Carrying them into the package would mean a duplicate tree | [[hmd-0024#where-tests-live-and-what-they-answer-for]] |
| The same gate commands are written in the repository guide and in each tool's guide | The cost of per-tool guides, which removed a worse problem: one guide describing tools it did not live beside. Each tool's guide is authoritative for its own commands; nothing enforces the duplication, and a stale command is a silent defect | [[hmd-0024#a-tool-s-front-matter-is-its-own]] |
| Installing under the retired name keeps installing the last release published under it, forever, and the retired plugin key survives as an alias | PyPI has no rename. The alias appears in site configs this project does not own, where removing it is a hard build failure whose cause is invisible to the site's author | [[hmd-0005#the-plugin-key-and-the-alias-that-keeps-existing-sites-building]] |
| The retired marketplace publisher and an unregistered npm scope are left as they are | Releasing a publisher name back into the pool lets an impostor take it. Deliberate inaction, not an oversight | [[hmd-0005#the-extension-s-identity-on-both-galleries]] |
| Changelog entries keep the old name where they record what a release was actually called | A changelog is a historical record | [[hmd-0005#what-deliberately-keeps-its-current-spelling]] |

## Done

- **`location_path` names the namespace root** — the setting formerly called
  `wiki`, read relative to the vault with the default `.`, the vault itself,
  instead of `doc/wiki`; no alias. `hmd init --location-path` replaces
  `--wiki`, and with no flag creates no directory; released in 0.5.0.
- **The HMD007 check follows only the embedded region** — it walks the
  section a fragment expands to, not every embed in the target card, so two
  embeds of different sections of one card, or a section embedding a sibling
  section, are no longer a false cycle. A card or a section that embeds itself
  still is. Held by the `embed-sections-of-one-card` and `embed-self` corpus
  cases; released in 0.5.0.
- **The corrected resolution algorithm** — `..` is normalised in every form,
  leaving the namespace is HMD003 in every phase (a nested vault and a symbolic
  link out of the root included), a written card extension is honoured in
  every phase, a named import of another extension falls through, `[[./]]` and
  `[[../]]` reach only folder notes, every imported search path is probed so
  HMD016 names each shadowed card once, and HMD012 is a warning. The eighteen
  `resolve-*` corpus cases, written from the specification, stand behind it;
  released in 0.5.0 [issue 0020].
- **The HyperMarkDown 0.1 rules** — heading fragments match by key, so
  headings in every script are linkable [issue 0014], setext
  headings are headings, HMD019 reports undecodable files, a byte-order mark is
  ignored, all three line endings are lines, names compare after NFC (import
  aliases included), offsets count UTF-8 bytes and columns code points, only
  `.hmd/` marks a vault, an anchor on a heading embeds its section, an anchored
  repeat heading is not HMD011, and block anchors are no longer rendered — the
  tutorial's "you cannot see it" paragraph showed its own. A determinism test
  and six corpus cases stand behind it; released in 0.5.0.
- **A syntax highlighter for cards** — a code sample marked `hmd` is coloured
  as a card wherever Pygments is used: links, embeds, block anchors, and
  frontmatter stand out, and a fenced block inside the sample is coloured as
  its own language.
- **The format, entire** — masking and the link grammar, heading links
  rewritten to the id Python-Markdown's table of contents gives the heading
  they matched, so links and rendered anchors cannot disagree, the four-phase
  resolution search — named imports, the spine, imported search paths, then
  the whole-tree sweep — folder notes, both import forms, and embed expansion
  with a depth cap and cycle detection.
- **Markdown cards** — `.md` and `.markdown` files load as cards that are not
  scanned, bind after `.hmd`, and are reported as HMD018 when shadowed; the MkDocs
  plugin links to the page MkDocs builds from one and never registers it; a
  heading right below the frontmatter fence is a heading again [HMD-0029].
- **`hmd lint`** — nineteen diagnostics with stable identifiers, JSON output, and
  exit codes a CI job can read.
- **`hmd render`** — flat markdown and HTML.
- **`hmd init`** — writes the project marker with every setting at its default,
  creates the namespace root it names, refuses to overwrite an existing config,
  and warns when an enclosing project would silently win.
- **The `[hql]` and `[semantics]` tables are left alone** — a configuration
  holding them loads and lints as one without them, whatever their values, and
  a test holds that [[hmd-0028#the-hql-table]].
- **The MkDocs plugin** — cards become pages, folder notes collapse onto their
  directory's URL, links are rewritten, red links render as red links without
  failing the build, embeds expand, and diagrams render through `d2`.
- **Publication** — opt-in visibility inherited from folder notes, unpublished
  cards absent from the built site entirely, and a warning when a published card
  reaches into one that is not.
- **Released and renamed** — on PyPI through a trusted publisher, under a name
  that now matches the domain, the npm scope, and the extension publisher, with
  the old entry point kept working as an alias.
- **The repository layout** — five tools under `tools/`, each owning its own
  front matter with no packaging symlinks, the shared corpus at the root where
  it belongs to every implementation, and a workspace root that is not itself a
  package.
- **A nested vault is its own vault** — a directory below the root carrying its
  own `.hmd/` is skipped when the workspace loads, so linting, the graph,
  rendering, and the site see none of its cards, and an import into it is
  HMD015. Ships in 0.3.1 [issue 0009]
- **A folder note's ordinary links land on the built site** — the plugin
  registers a folder note one directory above its file, and now resolves every
  relative Markdown link in it from the file's own directory before rewriting
  it for the registered page: links to cards, to plain pages, and to images,
  inline and as reference definitions. Ships in 0.3.1 [issue 0010]
- **The shared corpus runs here too** — every conformance case is checked
  against this implementation, the TypeScript one, and the Rust crate, so drift
  is caught whichever side moves. This one carries no expected-failure ledger,
  and its expected results are written from the specification, not generated
  from this code.

## Gates

```bash
uv sync --locked
uv run python -m pytest
uv run hmd lint --root doc/wiki --strict
uv run hmd lint --root examples/small              # exit 0, exactly one warning
uv run hmd lint --root examples/cs-alg-sorting --strict
uv run mkdocs build --strict
uv build --package HyperMarkDown
```

The one warning over `examples/small` is the deliberate red link that shows what
an unwritten page looks like. A clean run there is a regression, not an
improvement.

## Open questions

| Question | References |
| --- | --- |
| Is the language server a subcommand of the existing command, or its own script? | [[hmd-0024#the-language-server-is-python-on-pygls]] |
| Is its library a base dependency or an optional extra? An extra keeps the plain install small; a base dependency means the server exists wherever the command does | [[hmd-0024#the-language-server-is-python-on-pygls]] |
| Which language features move to the server first, and how does an editor report an absent server without implying the preview failed? That the extension becomes a client of it was settled; the sequencing was not | [[hmd-0024#the-language-server-is-python-on-pygls]] |
| Should `hmd graph` record each card's resolved search path, so a consumer can see what a card reaches without replaying the algorithm? | [[hmd-0001#5-link-resolution]] |
| When is the retired plugin alias withdrawn, and what announces it — a major version, a warning at build time, or nothing? | [[hmd-0005#the-plugin-key-and-the-alias-that-keeps-existing-sites-building]] |
| Does pruning the abandoned PyPI project mean yanking its releases or deleting it? Deletion frees the name for anyone to register and serve to a stale pin | [[hmd-0005#the-python-distribution-its-module-and-the-project-left-behind]] |
| Does the retired domain redirect permanently, or for a fixed window after which the registration is allowed to lapse? A lapsed domain that once served documentation is one someone else can serve anything from | [[hmd-0005#the-canonical-host-and-the-state-dns-has-to-reach]] |
| Should `hmd init` write a starter card in the root it creates? It creates an empty directory today, which lints clean and shows an author nothing — but a card written by a tool is a card somebody has to delete | [[hmd-0001#7-hmd-cli]] |
| Should `init` grow flags for the settings it writes, or is editing the file it just wrote the right affordance? | [[hmd-0001#7-hmd-cli]] |
| Should the linter check a namespace name that does not match the address form? Nothing reads that key yet, and the first thing to read it would be the first thing to break | [[hmd-0004#the-address-form]] |
| Should the specification's version sentence stop being what the repository guard parses, now that a rename has shown the sentence can move? | [[hmd-0005#the-wiki-card-that-carries-the-project-s-name]] |

## Changelog

- 2026-11-24: a Done row for reading `location_path`, the `wiki` setting's new name.
- 2026-11-22: the 0.1 rules are implemented, unreleased.
- 2026-11-22: the diagnostic count, eighteen, matches the code. Corrected on
  2026-11-24: it is nineteen, with HMD019.
- 2026-09-14: rewritten in the shape the trackers now use — status first in
  prose, then open work, broken, gaps, and done last. Work-point numbers are
  gone: a row is named by what it is. The header now says what HyperMarkDown is,
  why the canonical implementation is a Python package, and what that package
  holds.
- 2026-09-14: created by folding four per-proposal trackers into one file about
  the tool. Language questions moved to the format's tracker and the site's rows
  to the website's; the corpus runner moved in from the editor line's tracker,
  where it had been filed beside the rows that cannot deliver it.
- 2026-11-15: a Done row for the `[hql]` and `[semantics]` tables of
  `.hmd/config.toml`, which this tool does not read and does not reject.
- 2026-11-22: an Open row for property-based tests of the resolver's claims
  over generated vaults, the cheap form of the Lean model the format's tracker
  parks.
- 2026-11-22: Open rows for dropping the `.git` root fallback and for drawing
  only diagram cells in the site build.
- 2026-11-24: before the 0.1.0 release. The tool conforms to HyperMarkDown
  0.1.0, and is the reference implementation rather than the arbiter: the
  specification settles disagreements. The Open rows for the determinism
  test, HMD019 and the encoding rules, setext headings, and the `.git`
  fallback, and the Broken row for non-Latin headings, were already done and
  folded into the Done row for the 0.1 rules. Done rows for the corrected
  resolution algorithm and for the HMD007 check; the question about
  canonicity under a third implementation is answered by the specification's
  authority rule.
